"Henry, wait!" says Doyle "let's start a fire first!"
Everybody agrees. After picking dry branches, that lied everywhere, you build a small fire. Doyle bends over it and cracks with a lighter. Dry wood ignited easily; now fire merrily cracks by the entrance of your flimsy shelter.
It's getting dark very soon. In a few hours you can see only shelter's nearest trees, lit by the fire, and all the rest behind them was hidden by pitch-darkness.
You all tired and scared, but it's difficult to get to sleep. The jungle outside is full of weird and frightful sounds. Unknown animals make frightened screeches and howl in the thickets, you can often hear obscure rustles. Some apelike creatures came down the trees and look at your fire with curiosity. You look at them.
Suddenly these creatures begin to scream loud, being frightened. One by one they rush up the lianas with lightning speed. For a moment, silence fell upon the jungle. Next, branches on the forest skirt are being pushed apart and a large, hairy, night-black beast steps out to the glade.
Long hair hanged down his sides by locks, saber long fangs gleamed in light of fire. The beast stood still looking at your fire. His nostrils move, he obviously scents you.
The predator comes closer, then stops hesitating. It looked as if some unseen barrier restrained him. He is certainly afraid of fire, but your shelter is so unreliable... You've got to do something, while it's not too late.  